<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhodes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rhodes is an island in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>, off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, long valued as a naval and commercial base. In the seventh century it became a target of the first Arab fleets built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiyah</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> list Rhodes, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the Aegean, among the Byzantine territories raided by early Umayyad naval forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhodes.jpg" alt="Rhodes" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Union, Sentinel-2 Imagery · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodes_sentinel2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early Antigonids were also great naval powers: Demetrius won the sea battle off Salamis in 306 BCE, besieged <b>Rhodes</b> in 305–304 BCE with enormous siege engines, and built some of the largest warships of the age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It founded the Second Athenian League in 378 BCE, though the revolt of Chios, <b>Rhodes</b>, Cos and Byzantium in the Social War of 357–355 BCE weakened it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Aided by Athenian fleets under Chares and Phocion and by Chios, <b>Rhodes</b> and Kos, Byzantium held out and Philip withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rhodes</b>’s ambitions, launched from the Cape, drove British expansion north of the Limpopo into the Rhodesias.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 88 BCE he overran the Roman province of Asia and sent armies into Greece, but failed to take <b>Rhodes</b>, and Sulla defeated his generals at Chaeronea and Orchomenus in 86–85 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the second century Knossos and Gortyn alternately cooperated and fought, while Cretan cities clashed with <b>Rhodes</b> in the Cretan War of about 205–200 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Military</a></li></ul></section>
